    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-35178",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228361/assets/2280a258ec2cf548a5393772e497bece3ecbb61549c0d7260bad2146a9f3a094.png)\n\nThe graph of $f(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of ${f}^{-1}(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228361/assets/b2f729211c24879686601523f74935332dccbd6bf2e89b63af794b70dc721b1d.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,3) & ⟶(3,1) \\\\ (\\sqrt{3},9) & ⟶(9,\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
